FIG_7_1_HTML = '''<figure class="diagram-figure"><p class="diagram-scroll-hint">Swipe horizontally to view the full diagram.</p><svg aria-labelledby="day7-ssh-title day7-ssh-desc" role="img" viewbox="0 0 940 280" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><title id="day7-ssh-title">SSH client, bastion and private target path</title><desc id="day7-ssh-desc">An administrative client establishes an encrypted transport session through an intermediary bastion jump host via ProxyJump, terminating cryptographic authentication directly on the target private backend VM without storing credentials on the jump host.</desc><defs><marker id="day7-ssh-arrow" markerheight="8" markerwidth="10" orient="auto" refx="9" refy="4"><path d="M0,0 L10,4 L0,8 Z" fill="#38bdf8"></path></marker></defs><g fill="#121526" stroke-width="2"><rect height="130" rx="10" stroke="#38bdf8" width="220" x="20" y="40"></rect><image href="../assets/icons/generic/client.svg" x="32" y="52" width="24" height="24" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"/><rect height="130" rx="10" stroke="#f59e0b" width="260" x="310" y="40"></rect><image href="../assets/icons/gcp/core/compute-engine.svg" x="322" y="52" width="24" height="24" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"/><rect height="130" rx="10" stroke="#34d399" width="250" x="640" y="40"></rect><image href="../assets/icons/gcp/core/compute-engine.svg" x="652" y="52" width="24" height="24" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"/></g><g fill="#fce7f3" font-size="15" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle"><text x="130" y="75">Admin Client</text><text x="440" y="75">Bastion / Jump Host</text><text x="765" y="75">Private Backend VM</text></g><g fill="#a9b7cb" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle"><text x="130" y="108">ed25519 private key</text><text x="130" y="136">ProxyJump bastion</text><text x="440" y="108">public IP / TCP 22</text><text x="440" y="136">stdio TCP stream relay</text><text x="765" y="108">internal 10.128.0.5:22</text><text x="765" y="136">end-to-end crypto auth</text></g><g fill="none" marker-end="url(#day7-ssh-arrow)" stroke="#38bdf8" stroke-width="2"><path d="M240 105 L310 105"></path><path d="M570 105 L640 105"></path></g><text fill="#a9b7cb" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" x="470" y="220">ProxyJump routes TCP streams without exposing client private keys or decrypting payloads on the bastion.</text></svg><figcaption>Figure 7.1: Conceptual ProxyJump path with separate identity decisions at both hosts. It does not assert that either network path or account is configured.</figcaption></figure>'''

FIG_7_2_HTML = '''<figure class="diagram-figure"><p class="diagram-scroll-hint">Swipe horizontally to view the full diagram.</p><svg aria-labelledby="day7-pkg-title day7-pkg-desc" role="img" viewbox="0 0 940 280" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><title id="day7-pkg-title">Package metadata, installation and runtime verification</title><desc id="day7-pkg-desc">The OS package manager fetches release metadata from upstream repositories, cryptographically validates the release signature with a trusted local GPG keyring, resolves transitive dependencies, and applies transactional filesystem updates.</desc><defs><marker id="day7-pkg-arrow" markerheight="8" markerwidth="10" orient="auto" refx="9" refy="4"><path d="M0,0 L10,4 L0,8 Z" fill="#38bdf8"></path></marker></defs><g fill="#121526" stroke-width="2"><rect height="130" rx="10" stroke="#38bdf8" width="230" x="20" y="40"></rect><image href="../assets/icons/generic/server.svg" x="32" y="52" width="24" height="24" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"/><rect height="130" rx="10" stroke="#f43f5e" width="280" x="310" y="40"></rect><image href="../assets/icons/generic/policy.svg" x="322" y="52" width="24" height="24" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"/><rect height="130" rx="10" stroke="#34d399" width="250" x="650" y="40"></rect><image href="../assets/icons/generic/storage.svg" x="662" y="52" width="24" height="24" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"/></g><g fill="#fce7f3" font-size="15" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle"><text x="135" y="75">Upstream Repository</text><text x="450" y="75">GPG &amp; Solver Engine</text><text x="775" y="75">Local VFS Target</text></g><g fill="#a9b7cb" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle"><text x="135" y="108">InRelease / repomd.xml</text><text x="135" y="136">deb / rpm binary packages</text><text x="450" y="108">detached signature check</text><text x="450" y="136">SAT dependency tree solve</text><text x="775" y="108">unpack binaries &amp; libs</text><text x="775" y="136">ldconfig &amp; postinst hook</text></g><g fill="none" marker-end="url(#day7-pkg-arrow)" stroke="#38bdf8" stroke-width="2"><path d="M250 105 L310 105"></path><path d="M590 105 L650 105"></path></g><text fill="#a9b7cb" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" x="470" y="220">Package manager validation fails closed if the repository cryptographic signature does not match trusted keys.</text></svg><figcaption>Figure 7.2: The package lifecycle separates metadata refresh, host mutation and runtime proof. Exact commands and restart behavior depend on the selected distribution and package.</figcaption></figure>'''

FIG_7_3_HTML = '''<figure class="diagram-figure"><p class="diagram-scroll-hint">Swipe horizontally to view the full diagram.</p><svg aria-labelledby="day7-sh-title day7-sh-desc" role="img" viewbox="0 0 940 280" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><title id="day7-sh-title">Quoted shell arguments and validation exit gate</title><desc id="day7-sh-desc">The shell tokenizer takes input text, executes variable and command expansion, enforces quote boundaries to prevent unintended word splitting, executes processes, and passes the exit code to downstream control logic.</desc><defs><marker id="day7-sh-arrow" markerheight="8" markerwidth="10" orient="auto" refx="9" refy="4"><path d="M0,0 L10,4 L0,8 Z" fill="#38bdf8"></path></marker></defs><g fill="#121526" stroke-width="2"><rect height="130" rx="10" stroke="#38bdf8" width="220" x="20" y="40"></rect><image href="../assets/icons/generic/artifact.svg" x="32" y="52" width="24" height="24" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"/><rect height="130" rx="10" stroke="#f59e0b" width="300" x="290" y="40"></rect><image href="../assets/icons/generic/decision.svg" x="302" y="52" width="24" height="24" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"/><rect height="130" rx="10" stroke="#34d399" width="250" x="650" y="40"></rect><image href="../assets/icons/generic/outcome.svg" x="662" y="52" width="24" height="24" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"/></g><g fill="#fce7f3" font-size="15" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle"><text x="130" y="75">Input Parameters</text><text x="440" y="75">Shell Quoting &amp; Expansion</text><text x="775" y="75">Process Execution</text></g><g fill="#a9b7cb" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle"><text x="130" y="108">JSON strings &amp; env vars</text><text x="130" y="136">whitespace in arguments</text><text x="440" y="108">double-quotes preserve IFS</text><text x="440" y="136">unquoted expands to argv[]</text><text x="775" y="108">exact argument passing</text><text x="775" y="136">exit code 0 vs non-zero</text></g><g fill="none" marker-end="url(#day7-sh-arrow)" stroke="#38bdf8" stroke-width="2"><path d="M240 105 L290 105"></path><path d="M590 105 L650 105"></path></g><text fill="#a9b7cb" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" x="470" y="220">Proper double quoting preserves single semantic arguments across internal whitespace boundaries.</text></svg><figcaption>Figure 7.3: The script contract preserves arguments and carries validation failure to the caller. It depicts control flow, not measured pipeline behavior.</figcaption></figure>'''

FIG_7_4_HTML = '''<figure class="diagram-figure"><p class="diagram-scroll-hint">Swipe horizontally to view the full diagram.</p><svg aria-labelledby="day7-proc-title day7-proc-desc" role="img" viewbox="0 0 940 280" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><title id="day7-proc-title">Service unit, process tree and proc evidence</title><desc id="day7-proc-desc">The Linux kernel maintains internal task structures in memory and projects live process states, open file descriptors, and resource limits into the /proc pseudo-filesystem on demand without disk I/O.</desc><defs><marker id="day7-proc-arrow" markerheight="8" markerwidth="10" orient="auto" refx="9" refy="4"><path d="M0,0 L10,4 L0,8 Z" fill="#38bdf8"></path></marker></defs><g fill="#121526" stroke-width="2"><rect height="130" rx="10" stroke="#38bdf8" width="240" x="20" y="40"></rect><image href="../assets/icons/gcp/core/compute-engine.svg" x="32" y="52" width="24" height="24" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"/><rect height="130" rx="10" stroke="#a78bfa" width="290" x="300" y="40"></rect><image href="../assets/icons/generic/storage.svg" x="312" y="52" width="24" height="24" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"/><rect height="130" rx="10" stroke="#34d399" width="250" x="650" y="40"></rect><image href="../assets/icons/generic/monitoring.svg" x="662" y="52" width="24" height="24" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"/></g><g fill="#fce7f3" font-size="15" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle"><text x="140" y="75">Kernel task_struct</text><text x="445" y="75">/proc VFS Projection</text><text x="775" y="75">Observability Tools</text></g><g fill="#a9b7cb" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle"><text x="140" y="108">PID, PPID, state registers</text><text x="140" y="136">files_struct &amp; mm_struct</text><text x="445" y="108">/proc/[pid]/status &amp; cmdline</text><text x="445" y="136">/proc/[pid]/fd/* links</text><text x="775" y="108">ps, pgrep, pstree</text><text x="775" y="136">synthetic monitoring parsers</text></g><g fill="none" marker-end="url(#day7-proc-arrow)" stroke="#38bdf8" stroke-width="2"><path d="M260 105 L300 105"></path><path d="M590 105 L650 105"></path></g><text fill="#a9b7cb" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" x="470" y="220">Reading /proc files triggers kernel handler functions to render live in-memory telemetry as text streams.</text></svg><figcaption>Figure 7.4: The supplied PIDs illustrate the evidence relationship only. A live diagnosis must read the current unit, process tree and permitted /proc paths.</figcaption></figure>'''

FIG_7_5_HTML = '''<figure class="diagram-figure"><p class="diagram-scroll-hint">Swipe horizontally to view the full incident diagram.</p><svg aria-labelledby="d7-ssh-inc-title d7-ssh-inc-desc" role="img" viewbox="0 0 940 330" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><title id="d7-ssh-inc-title">Broken and corrected SSH jump-host paths</title><desc id="d7-ssh-inc-desc">The failing path reaches the bastion but lacks a configured or permitted final hop, while an unsafe public rule exposes the target. The corrected path restores the client to bastion to private VM route and removes public reachability.</desc><defs><marker id="d7-ssh-inc-arrow" markerheight="8" markerwidth="10" orient="auto" refx="9" refy="4"><path d="M0 0L10 4L0 8Z" fill="#38bdf8"></path></marker></defs><text fill="#f43f5e" font-weight="700" x="20" y="28">BEFORE · bastion reached, final hop broken, public rule added</text><text fill="#34d399" font-weight="700" x="20" y="185">AFTER · intended private path restored and verified</text><g fill="#121526" stroke-width="2"><rect height="90" rx="10" stroke="#38bdf8" width="220" x="25" y="48"></rect><image href="../assets/icons/generic/client.svg" x="35" y="58" width="22" height="22" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"/><rect height="90" rx="10" stroke="#38bdf8" width="220" x="360" y="48"></rect><image href="../assets/icons/gcp/core/compute-engine.svg" x="370" y="58" width="22" height="22" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"/><rect height="90" rx="10" stroke="#f43f5e" width="220" x="695" y="48"></rect><image href="../assets/icons/generic/failure.svg" x="705" y="58" width="22" height="22" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"/><rect height="90" rx="10" stroke="#38bdf8" width="220" x="25" y="205"></rect><image href="../assets/icons/generic/client.svg" x="35" y="215" width="22" height="22" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"/><rect height="90" rx="10" stroke="#34d399" width="220" x="360" y="205"></rect><image href="../assets/icons/gcp/core/compute-engine.svg" x="370" y="215" width="22" height="22" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"/><rect height="90" rx="10" stroke="#34d399" width="220" x="695" y="205"></rect><image href="../assets/icons/generic/outcome.svg" x="705" y="215" width="22" height="22" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"/></g><g fill="#fce7f3" text-anchor="middle"><text x="135" y="82">Client</text><text x="470" y="82">Bastion reached</text><text x="805" y="82">Private VM · BROKEN</text><text x="135" y="239">Client + ProxyJump</text><text x="470" y="239">Bastion path allowed</text><text x="805" y="239">Private VM · VERIFIED</text></g><g marker-end="url(#d7-ssh-inc-arrow)" stroke="#38bdf8" stroke-width="2"><path d="M245 95L354 95"></path><path d="M580 95L689 95" stroke-dasharray="7 5"></path><path d="M245 252L354 252"></path><path d="M580 252L689 252"></path></g><text fill="#a9b7cb" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" x="470" y="320">Verify both host identities and remove the accidental public ingress rule.</text></svg><figcaption>Figure 7.5: Supplied incident example. Dashed final-hop routing marks the failed path; the corrected row keeps the target private. Actual firewall and host-key evidence must come from the environment.</figcaption></figure>'''

FIG_7_6_HTML = '''<figure class="diagram-figure"><p class="diagram-scroll-hint">Swipe horizontally to view the full incident diagram.</p><svg aria-labelledby="d7-pkg-inc-title d7-pkg-inc-desc" role="img" viewbox="0 0 940 330" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><title id="d7-pkg-inc-title">Metadata refresh mistaken for a completed package patch</title><desc id="d7-pkg-inc-desc">Before repair, repository metadata is current but the installed and running versions remain vulnerable. After repair, an approved transaction changes the installed package and runtime verification confirms the intended process version.</desc><defs><marker id="d7-pkg-inc-arrow" markerheight="8" markerwidth="10" orient="auto" refx="9" refy="4"><path d="M0 0L10 4L0 8Z" fill="#38bdf8"></path></marker></defs><text fill="#f43f5e" font-weight="700" x="20" y="28">BEFORE · metadata current ≠ package patched</text><text fill="#34d399" font-weight="700" x="20" y="185">AFTER · approved transaction plus runtime proof</text><g fill="#121526" stroke-width="2"><rect height="90" rx="10" stroke="#38bdf8" width="260" x="25" y="48"></rect><image href="../assets/icons/generic/server.svg" x="35" y="58" width="22" height="22" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"/><rect height="90" rx="10" stroke="#f43f5e" width="260" x="340" y="48"></rect><image href="../assets/icons/generic/storage.svg" x="350" y="58" width="22" height="22" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"/><rect height="90" rx="10" stroke="#f43f5e" width="260" x="655" y="48"></rect><image href="../assets/icons/generic/failure.svg" x="665" y="58" width="22" height="22" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"/><rect height="90" rx="10" stroke="#38bdf8" width="260" x="25" y="205"></rect><image href="../assets/icons/generic/server.svg" x="35" y="215" width="22" height="22" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"/><rect height="90" rx="10" stroke="#34d399" width="260" x="340" y="205"></rect><image href="../assets/icons/generic/policy.svg" x="350" y="215" width="22" height="22" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"/><rect height="90" rx="10" stroke="#34d399" width="260" x="655" y="205"></rect><image href="../assets/icons/generic/outcome.svg" x="665" y="215" width="22" height="22" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"/></g><g fill="#fce7f3" text-anchor="middle"><text x="155" y="82">Candidate refreshed</text><text x="470" y="82">Installed: old</text><text x="785" y="82">Process: vulnerable</text><text x="155" y="239">Candidate compared</text><text x="470" y="239">Installed: target</text><text x="785" y="239">Process: verified</text></g><g marker-end="url(#d7-pkg-inc-arrow)" stroke="#38bdf8" stroke-width="2"><path d="M285 95L334 95"></path><path d="M600 95L649 95"></path><path d="M285 252L334 252"></path><path d="M600 252L649 252"></path></g><text fill="#a9b7cb" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" x="470" y="320">The acceptance boundary is the running process, not metadata-download output.</text></svg><figcaption>Figure 7.6: Supplied incident example separating discovery, installation and runtime state. Package names, versions and restart requirements must be measured on the target host.</figcaption></figure>'''

FIG_7_7_HTML = '''<figure class="diagram-figure"><p class="diagram-scroll-hint">Swipe horizontally to view the full incident diagram.</p><svg aria-labelledby="d7-shell-inc-title d7-shell-inc-desc" role="img" viewbox="0 0 940 330" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><title id="d7-shell-inc-title">Malformed JSON published before and blocked after script repair</title><desc id="d7-shell-inc-desc">Before repair, validation fails but a later successful print command sets exit zero and publishing continues. After repair, quoted inputs and an accumulated failure status return exit one and block publishing.</desc><defs><marker id="d7-shell-inc-arrow" markerheight="8" markerwidth="10" orient="auto" refx="9" refy="4"><path d="M0 0L10 4L0 8Z" fill="#38bdf8"></path></marker></defs><text fill="#f43f5e" font-weight="700" x="20" y="28">BEFORE · validator failure overwritten by exit 0</text><text fill="#34d399" font-weight="700" x="20" y="185">AFTER · quoted inputs and nonzero release gate</text><g fill="#121526" stroke-width="2"><rect height="90" rx="10" stroke="#f43f5e" width="260" x="25" y="48"></rect><image href="../assets/icons/generic/artifact.svg" x="35" y="58" width="22" height="22" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"/><rect height="90" rx="10" stroke="#f43f5e" width="260" x="340" y="48"></rect><image href="../assets/icons/generic/decision.svg" x="350" y="58" width="22" height="22" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"/><rect height="90" rx="10" stroke="#f43f5e" width="260" x="655" y="48"></rect><image href="../assets/icons/generic/failure.svg" x="665" y="58" width="22" height="22" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"/><rect height="90" rx="10" stroke="#38bdf8" width="260" x="25" y="205"></rect><image href="../assets/icons/generic/artifact.svg" x="35" y="215" width="22" height="22" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"/><rect height="90" rx="10" stroke="#34d399" width="260" x="340" y="205"></rect><image href="../assets/icons/generic/decision.svg" x="350" y="215" width="22" height="22" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"/><rect height="90" rx="10" stroke="#34d399" width="260" x="655" y="205"></rect><image href="../assets/icons/generic/outcome.svg" x="665" y="215" width="22" height="22" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"/></g><g fill="#fce7f3" text-anchor="middle"><text x="155" y="82">Malformed JSON</text><text x="470" y="82">Error then print succeeds</text><text x="785" y="82">PUBLISH · wrong</text><text x="155" y="239">Quoted "$@"</text><text x="470" y="239">failed=1 retained</text><text x="785" y="239">BLOCK · exit 1</text></g><g marker-end="url(#d7-shell-inc-arrow)" stroke="#38bdf8" stroke-width="2"><path d="M285 95L334 95"></path><path d="M600 95L649 95"></path><path d="M285 252L334 252"></path><path d="M600 252L649 252"></path></g><text fill="#a9b7cb" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" x="470" y="320">Verification observes both emitted messages and the caller-visible exit status.</text></svg><figcaption>Figure 7.7: Supplied incident example. The labels, not color alone, distinguish the incorrect publish path and corrected blocking gate. It does not represent a live release pipeline.</figcaption></figure>'''

FIG_7_8_HTML = '''<figure class="diagram-figure"><p class="diagram-scroll-hint">Swipe horizontally to view the full incident diagram.</p><svg aria-labelledby="d7-proc-inc-title d7-proc-inc-desc" role="img" viewbox="0 0 940 330" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><title id="d7-proc-inc-title">Wrong PID selection and corrected process-tree diagnosis</title><desc id="d7-proc-inc-desc">Before repair, a shared Python command name leads the operator to stop an unrelated worker. After repair, the service unit, parent PID, child PID, proc status and logs identify the stalled order worker before a supervisor action.</desc><defs><marker id="d7-proc-inc-arrow" markerheight="8" markerwidth="10" orient="auto" refx="9" refy="4"><path d="M0 0L10 4L0 8Z" fill="#38bdf8"></path></marker></defs><text fill="#f43f5e" font-weight="700" x="20" y="28">BEFORE · command-name match selects the wrong PID</text><text fill="#34d399" font-weight="700" x="20" y="185">AFTER · unit and process tree identify the intended worker</text><g fill="#121526" stroke-width="2"><rect height="90" rx="10" stroke="#38bdf8" width="260" x="25" y="48"></rect><image href="../assets/icons/generic/server.svg" x="35" y="58" width="22" height="22" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"/><rect height="90" rx="10" stroke="#f43f5e" width="260" x="340" y="48"></rect><image href="../assets/icons/generic/decision.svg" x="350" y="58" width="22" height="22" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"/><rect height="90" rx="10" stroke="#f43f5e" width="260" x="655" y="48"></rect><image href="../assets/icons/generic/failure.svg" x="665" y="58" width="22" height="22" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"/><rect height="90" rx="10" stroke="#38bdf8" width="260" x="25" y="205"></rect><image href="../assets/icons/gcp/core/compute-engine.svg" x="35" y="215" width="22" height="22" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"/><rect height="90" rx="10" stroke="#34d399" width="260" x="340" y="205"></rect><image href="../assets/icons/generic/storage.svg" x="350" y="215" width="22" height="22" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"/><rect height="90" rx="10" stroke="#34d399" width="260" x="655" y="205"></rect><image href="../assets/icons/generic/outcome.svg" x="665" y="215" width="22" height="22" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"/></g><g fill="#fce7f3" text-anchor="middle"><text x="155" y="82">Many "python" rows</text><text x="470" y="82">Wrong PID signaled</text><text x="785" y="82">Stall remains</text><text x="155" y="239">Unit → main PID</text><text x="470" y="239">PPid + /proc + logs</text><text x="785" y="239">Supervisor restart</text></g><g marker-end="url(#d7-proc-inc-arrow)" stroke="#38bdf8" stroke-width="2"><path d="M285 95L334 95"></path><path d="M600 95L649 95"></path><path d="M285 252L334 252"></path><path d="M600 252L649 252"></path></g><text fill="#a9b7cb" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" x="470" y="320">Recovery is accepted only after a synthetic order succeeds; a listed PID is insufficient.</text></svg><figcaption>Figure 7.8: Supplied incident example contrasting a name-only PID choice with supervisor-aware evidence. Live PIDs and descriptors are environment-specific and transient.</figcaption></figure>'''

PART1_HTML = '''<article class="topic-card overview" id="topic-01-overview">
<h3>SSH</h3>
<p><strong class="keyword">Secure Shell</strong> (SSH) provides cryptographically authenticated, encrypted transport sessions across untrusted networks. Beyond basic interactive terminal access, SSH underpins enterprise cloud access architectures through asymmetric key pairs, configuration file profiles, dynamic port forwarding, and multi-hop bastion jump proxies.</p>
<p><strong class="side-heading">Why today:</strong> Establishes secure administrative boundaries for private cloud infrastructure without exposing database sockets or internal management ports to the public internet.</p>
<p class="problem-preview">An administrative engineer enabling SSH agent forwarding across a shared staging jump host exposes their decrypted private key credentials to local root users on the bastion. Rogue operators on the compromised jump host hijack the forwarded socket to authenticate to high-security production database instances.</p>
</article>

<article class="topic-card overview" id="topic-02-overview">
<h3>Package managers (apt, yum/dnf)</h3>
<p><strong class="keyword">Package managers</strong> automate the installation, upgrade, dependency resolution, and cryptographically verified delivery of software packages across Linux distributions. Tools like APT for Debian/Ubuntu and YUM/DNF for Red Hat/CentOS/Rocky Linux query repository metadata, verify GPG signatures, and track system state in transactional databases.</p>
<p><strong class="side-heading">Why today:</strong> Provides predictable binary dependency trees and security patch distribution across fleets of virtual machine instances and immutable container base images.</p>
<p class="problem-preview">A production provisioning automation script fails during an auto-scaling event because an upstream third-party repository GPG key expired, halting system updates. The failed package manager transaction blocks VM startup, preventing replacement web server instances from handling incoming peak traffic.</p>
</article>

<article class="topic-card overview" id="topic-03-overview">
<h3>Shell scripting</h3>
<p><strong class="keyword">Shell scripting</strong> orchestrates operating system commands, data streams, and execution control flow into automated administrative tools and deployment pipelines. Mastering variable quoting, robust control loops, conditional evaluations, signal handling, and strict exit code propagation prevents silent execution failures and data corruption.</p>
<p><strong class="side-heading">Why today:</strong> Serves as the universal glue code for container entrypoints, continuous deployment automations, infrastructure preflight validations, and cloud VM startup scripts.</p>
<p class="problem-preview">An unquoted variable in an automated backup cleanup script subjects filenames with spaces to shell word splitting, executing recursive file deletion against unintended root directories. The resulting filesystem truncation destroys customer application configuration state across multiple service mount points.</p>
</article>

<article class="topic-card overview" id="topic-04-overview">
<h3>/proc virtual filesystem and process trees</h3>
<p><strong class="keyword">The /proc filesystem</strong> is a pseudo-filesystem generated dynamically by the Linux kernel in virtual memory to expose real-time process metadata, memory allocations, open file descriptors, and hardware telemetry. Process trees maintain parent-child ancestry, enabling operators to trace service lineages, inspect environment variables, and diagnose zombie or orphaned workloads.</p>
<p><strong class="side-heading">Why today:</strong> Supplies the underlying observability instrumentation utilized by container runtimes, monitoring agents, and cloud health check probes to measure system performance and diagnose execution faults.</p>
<p class="problem-preview">An unmonitored batch worker process deleted its primary multi-gigabyte log file from disk while retaining an active file descriptor, causing the VM root disk to reach 100% capacity despite du reporting free space. Application database transactions freeze due to disk exhaustion because the kernel refuses to reclaim unlinked inode blocks until the owning process terminates.</p>
</article>'''

